# rules: java-missing-auth
# languages: java
# fixture: java/CWE-306.java
# from: CWE-306 (Missing Authentication for Critical Function), CWE-287
#
# The rule reports a mapping in a file that protects some of its other
# endpoints, and says nothing about a file that protects none. An application
# with no method-level security has made a decision - the rules may be in a
# SecurityFilterChain, or there may be no authentication at all because it is a
# sample - and reporting every endpoint in it tells the reader nothing they did
# not know. What is a bug is the endpoint that was missed: `@PreAuthorize` on
# three methods of a controller and not on the fourth. Over Spring PetClinic,
# which has no security anywhere, that is the difference between sixteen
# findings and none.
#
# The guard is a regex rather than a pattern because Java's annotations are
# modifiers on the declaration they precede, so there is no node for
# `@PreAuthorize` on its own and `scan_ast` cannot see one. That is also why
# the unprotected mapping is written as `@GetMapping(...)` immediately above
# `public`: an annotation between the two is a different modifier list and does
# not match, which is what makes the protected methods below invisible to the
# sink half of this rule in the first place.
#
# There is one rule per language for this, and it reports under
# `<language>-missing-auth`. The corpus briefly carried two or three - an
# `-authn` spelling, an `-auth` spelling and this one - written against
# CWE-287, CWE-306 and CWE-862 but all asking the same question: is there an
# authentication guard on this endpoint. They fired on the same lines and a
# route with no middleware got three findings under three ids. Whether the
# missing guard is called authentication or authorization is not something the
# syntax says, so it is one finding; `<language>-862-rbac` is the separate rule
# for the endpoint that does authenticate and then checks no role.
#
# Spring controllers that expose @RequestMapping/@GetMapping etc without
# @PreAuthorize, @Secured or @RolesAllowed allow unauthenticated access.
# The rule finds request mappings that are not protected by an auth
# annotation; ok cases have @PreAuthorize or @Secured.

. as $root
| ($root | scan_regex("*.java";
    ["@(?:PreAuthorize|Secured|RolesAllowed|PermitAll|DenyAll)\\s*\\("])) as $protectedSomewhere
| ( ($root | scan_ast(["*.java"]; [
      "@RequestMapping($$$_)\npublic $R $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }",
      "@RequestMapping($$$_)\n$R $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }",
      "@GetMapping($$$_)\npublic $R $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }",
      "@PostMapping($$$_)\npublic $R $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }",
      "@PutMapping($$$_)\npublic $R $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }",
      "@DeleteMapping($$$_)\npublic $R $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }",
      "@PatchMapping($$$_)\npublic $R $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }"
    ])) as $all
    | ( $all | of(["@RequestMapping($$$_)\npublic $R $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }","@RequestMapping($$$_)\n$R $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }","@GetMapping($$$_)\npublic $R $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }","@PostMapping($$$_)\npublic $R $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }","@PutMapping($$$_)\npublic $R $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }","@DeleteMapping($$$_)\npublic $R $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }","@PatchMapping($$$_)\npublic $R $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }"])
          | in_files_with($protectedSomewhere)
        | finding("java-missing-auth";
            "this endpoint has no authentication annotation and its neighbours "
            + "in this file do; add @PreAuthorize or @Secured, or say why it is "
            + "deliberately open") ) )
| report
